{"id": "DfTvJarR7hO", "title": "Video by therishikatha", "description": "Was Kerala really pulled out of the sea by Parshurama's axe? 🌊🪓 Happy Kerala Piravi! 🌴\n\nThe Mahabharata (Shanti Parva 12.49) says that after Parshurama defeated the arrogant kings, he gave the whole earth away to Sage Kashyapa. Kashyapa told him he could no longer live on land he had given away, and the ocean made new land for him on the western shore.\n\nKerala's own legend, the Keralolpathi, tells how. At Gokarna he prayed to Varuna, the god of the sea, and threw his axe. The sea drew back as far as it flew, from Gokarna to Kanyakumari. That is why Kerala is called Parashurama Kshetram, the land of Parshurama.\n\nAnd science? Kerala's coastal plain is young land. Thousands of years ago the sea came further inland, and the shore has moved out since. The Vembanad backwaters began as a bay of the sea, shut in by sand bars, and Kuttanad is still farmed below sea level. The legend says the sea drew back. The land says so too.\n\n📚 Want stories like this for your kids? Our picture books of the gods, Books 1–3, are a set of 3 for ₹500 at rishikatha.com (link in bio).\n\n👇 Which part of Kerala are YOU from? Tell us in the comments!\n💾 Save this, and share it with a Malayali friend 🌴\n\nA Parshurama series is coming soon. Follow @therishikatha so you don't miss it 🪓\n\n#KeralaPiravi #Kerala #Parashurama #Parshuram #ParashuramaKshetram #GodsOwnCountry #Keralolpathi #Mahabharata #HinduMythology #IndianMythology #Gokarna #Kanyakumari #Kuttanad #Backwaters #SanatanDharma", "channel": "therishikatha", "uploader_id": "22569163881", "uploader": "Rishi Katha", "timestamp": 1793771208, "like_count": 13829, "comment_count": 224, "http_headers": {"Referer": "https://www.instagram.com/"}, "formats": [{"format_id": "dash-1874063574008564a", "ext": "m4a", "tbr": 54.468, "asr": 48000, "format_note": "DASH audio", "container": "m4a_dash", "vcodec": "none", "acodec": "mp4a.40.5", "url": "https://instagram.fblr2-2.fn